from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The First World War began in which year?","options":["1912","1914","1917","1918"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The war began in 1914, after the assassination in Sarajevo in June of that year, and ended with the armistice of 11 November 1918."},{"prompt":"The Bolshevik revolution that brought Lenin''s party to power took place in…","options":["1905","1917","1921","1924"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The October Revolution of 1917 brought the Bolsheviks to power; Lenin died in 1924 and Stalin later took control."},{"prompt":"The Wall Street Crash that opened the Great Depression happened in…","options":["1919","1925","1929","1933"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The crash of October 1929 triggered a worldwide depression through the 1930s."},{"prompt":"World War II in Europe ended in which year?","options":["1943","1944","1945","1946"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Germany surrendered on 8 May 1945; Japan surrendered that August, ending the war in the Pacific."},{"prompt":"The first atomic bombs were dropped on Hiroshima and Nagasaki in…","options":["1943","1945","1947","1949"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The bombs fell in August 1945; the Soviet Union tested its own atomic bomb in 1949, opening the arms race."},{"prompt":"India became independent from British rule in…","options":["1945","1947","1950","1960"],"answer":1,"explanation":"India and Pakistan became independent in 1947, an early milestone in the postwar wave of decolonization."},{"prompt":"The Berlin Wall fell in…","options":["1985","1989","1991","1993"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The wall fell on 9 November 1989, as communist governments across Eastern Europe collapsed."},{"prompt":"The Soviet Union was formally dissolved in…","options":["1988","1990","1991","1993"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Soviet Union dissolved in December 1991, ending the Cold War."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'The world in 1900: empires and industrial power', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe how a small group of industrial powers dominated the globe in 1900, explain where their wealth and weapons came from, and locate the main empires on a world map. By the end you will be able to say why 1900 was both a high point of European confidence and a moment of deep strain. Warm-up: before reading, guess which single empire governed roughly a quarter of the world''s people and land in 1900. Think about why industrial strength and imperial reach tended to grow together, and keep that link in mind as we test it against real figures. You will also start to see the world from outside Europe, asking how people in China, India, Africa, and Latin America experienced the same decades. That global view is the spine of this whole course, so practice it from the first lesson. Hold your guess about the largest empire; the answer appears in the next section, along with the forces that made such empires possible and the pressures already working to pull them apart."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"In 1900 the world was shaped by industrial power and empire. The Second Industrial Revolution, running from about 1870 to 1914, had spread steel, electricity, chemicals, railways, and the telegraph across Europe, North America, and Japan. The states that mastered these technologies, among them Britain, Germany, France, the United States, Russia, and a fast-rising Japan, turned factory output into navies, armies, and overseas colonies. Empire and industry reinforced each other: colonies supplied cotton, rubber, oil, and minerals, and they bought manufactured goods in return. The British Empire alone governed roughly a quarter of the planet''s land and people. Yet this order was global and uneven. The Qing in China, the Ottomans, and other older states had been pressed into unequal treaties and had lost territory. Meanwhile ideas of nationalism, socialism, and anti-colonial resistance were spreading among the very populations that empire tried to control. The world of 1900 looked stable and confident from London or Berlin, but beneath the surface it was tense, competitive, and primed for conflict."},{"heading":"Key events & causes","text":"Several developments defined the period. The Berlin Conference of 1884 to 1885 set rules for the European scramble for Africa, and by 1900 nearly the entire continent, except Ethiopia and Liberia, was under European control. Japan''s Meiji Restoration of 1868 launched rapid industrialization, and in 1904 to 1905 Japan defeated Russia, the first modern victory of an Asian power over a European one. In China the Boxer Uprising of 1900 was crushed by an eight-nation force, deepening foreign domination of the Qing. The United States, having industrialized quickly after its Civil War, took the Philippines and other territories in 1898. The causes behind all this were linked: industrial economies needed raw materials and markets, rival nations competed for prestige and strategic bases, and new weapons made conquest cheaper for the industrial powers. Rising nationalism, both among great powers and among colonized peoples, added pressure. By 1900 world population stood near 1.6 billion, and global trade had never been denser, or more unequal."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which empire governed about a quarter of the world in 1900? 2) What two decades roughly bound the Second Industrial Revolution? 3) Which conference set rules for the partition of Africa? 4) Which Asian power defeated Russia in 1904 to 1905, and what made that notable? 5) Name two African states that stayed independent in 1900. Answers: 1) The British Empire. 2) About 1870 to 1914. 3) The Berlin Conference of 1884 to 1885. 4) Japan; it was the first modern defeat of a European great power by an Asian state, showing that industrialization was not uniquely Western. 5) Ethiopia and Liberia. Bonus: explain in one sentence why industry and empire grew together. A strong answer notes that factories needed raw materials and markets, while colonies supplied both, so economic and imperial power fed each other and pushed the great powers into ever sharper rivalry."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: imagining 1900 as a peaceful golden age. It was prosperous for industrial elites but built on conquest, forced labor, and sharp inequality, and rival powers were already arming for war. Mistake: treating industrialization as purely European. Japan, the United States, and parts of Russia industrialized fast, and colonized societies had their own economies and resistance movements. Mistake: assuming empires were permanent. Within fifty years most would be gone. Keep a global lens: for every event in a European capital, ask how it was experienced in Lagos, Delhi, Shanghai, or Buenos Aires. Exit ticket: (a) Name the two African states that remained independent in 1900. (b) In one phrase, why did industrial power and empire tend to grow together? Answers: (a) Ethiopia and Liberia. (b) Industry needed raw materials and markets, and empire supplied both, so each strengthened the other."}]}'::jsonb, null, 14),
  (3, 'The Great War: World War I and its aftermath', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain why a regional assassination in 1914 escalated into a global war, trace the main fronts, and describe how the peace of 1919 reshaped the world. By the end you will be able to list the long-term causes of the First World War and connect them to its enormous consequences. Warm-up: before reading, name one alliance system you think helped turn a local crisis into a continental war. Keep in mind that this was a world war, not only a European one: battles were fought in Africa and the Middle East, and millions of colonial soldiers and laborers served far from home. As you read, track how the war toppled four empires and planted seeds for later conflict, including the Second World War. Hold one question in mind throughout: did the 1919 settlement secure peace, or set up the next war? We will return to it in the exit ticket, once you have seen both the fighting and the fragile peace that followed."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The First World War, fought from 1914 to 1918, grew out of four deep pressures often summarized as militarism, alliances, imperialism, and nationalism. Europe''s great powers had divided into two armed camps: the Triple Alliance of Germany, Austria-Hungary, and Italy, and the Triple Entente of France, Russia, and Britain. Years of imperial rivalry and an arms race, especially the naval contest between Britain and Germany, had made every crisis dangerous. When war came, new industrial weapons, machine guns, heavy artillery, and poison gas, produced slaughter on a scale no one expected. On the Western Front, armies dug into trenches and barely moved for years while casualties mounted. The war was global in reach: fighting spread to the Ottoman Middle East, to Germany''s African colonies, and across the oceans, and Britain and France drew heavily on troops from India, Africa, and the wider empire. It was total war, mobilizing whole economies and societies, and it left Europe exhausted and transformed."},{"heading":"Key events & causes","text":"The spark came on 28 June 1914, when Gavrilo Princip, a Bosnian Serb nationalist, assassinated Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria-Hungary in Sarajevo. Through the alliance system, local anger became continental war within weeks. Germany''s advance through Belgium pulled in Britain. The Western Front froze into trench deadlock, while the Eastern Front stayed more mobile. Key turning points included the entry of the United States in 1917, after German submarine warfare and the Zimmermann Telegram, and the exit of Russia following its 1917 revolution, confirmed by the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk in 1918. Fighting ended with the armistice of 11 November 1918. The human cost was staggering: roughly 16 to 20 million dead. Closely overlapping, the influenza pandemic of 1918 to 1919 killed tens of millions more worldwide. The Treaty of Versailles in 1919 forced Germany to accept blame and heavy reparations, created the League of Nations, and redrew borders, while former Ottoman lands became European-run mandates across the Middle East."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Give the day, month, and year of the Sarajevo assassination. 2) Name the two main alliance blocs. 3) In which year did the United States enter the war? 4) What treaty took Russia out of the war in 1918? 5) Roughly how many people died in the war itself? Answers: 1) 28 June 1914. 2) The Triple Alliance (Germany, Austria-Hungary, Italy) and the Triple Entente (France, Russia, Britain). 3) 1917. 4) The Treaty of Brest-Litovsk. 5) About 16 to 20 million. Bonus: which four empires collapsed as a result of the war? A full answer names the German, Austro-Hungarian, Russian, and Ottoman empires, whose fall redrew the maps of Europe and the Middle East. Note too that colonial troops from India and Africa fought and died in large numbers, a global cost that older accounts often leave out."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking the assassination alone caused the war. The killing was the trigger, but alliances, militarism, imperial rivalry, and nationalism were the loaded conditions that made it explode. Mistake: picturing the war as only European trenches. It was fought in Africa, the Middle East, and at sea, and colonial soldiers served in huge numbers. Mistake: believing Versailles brought lasting peace. Its harsh terms and unstable new borders fed German resentment and economic trouble, helping set the stage for fascism and a second war twenty years later. Keep the global frame and the long chain of causes in view. Exit ticket: (a) Which four empires collapsed because of the war? (b) Did the 1919 settlement secure peace or help cause the next war, and why? Answers: (a) German, Austro-Hungarian, Russian, and Ottoman. (b) It is widely judged to have helped cause the next war, because reparations, war guilt, and redrawn borders left deep grievances, especially in Germany."}]}'::jsonb, null, 14),
  (4, 'The Russian Revolution and the rise of communism', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain why the Russian monarchy collapsed in 1917, how the Bolsheviks seized and held power, and why these events mattered far beyond Russia. By the end you will be able to describe the two revolutions of 1917, the civil war that followed, and the birth of the Soviet Union. Warm-up: before reading, suggest one reason a world war might push a struggling empire into revolution. Keep the global angle in mind: the Russian Revolution gave communism its first state and inspired movements from China to Latin America for the rest of the century. As you read, separate the February and October events of 1917, which are easy to confuse, and notice how war, hunger, and land hunger combined to topple the Tsar. Hold one question: how did a small, disciplined party end up ruling the world''s largest country? We return to it in the exit ticket, after tracing how theory became a state and then a worldwide movement."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"By 1917 the Russian Empire was buckling under the strain of the First World War. Military defeats, food shortages, and anger at Tsar Nicholas II brought the monarchy down in the February Revolution, replaced by a weak Provisional Government that kept Russia in the war. Into this vacuum stepped the Bolsheviks, a Marxist party led by Vladimir Lenin, who promised peace, land, and bread, and power to the soviets, the workers'' and soldiers'' councils. In the October Revolution the Bolsheviks seized control of the capital. Their ideology, soon called Marxism-Leninism, held that a disciplined revolutionary party should lead workers to overthrow capitalism and build a classless society. In practice the new regime created a one-party state. A brutal civil war followed, and out of it emerged the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics. The revolution turned a European political theory into a governing power, and it offered a model that anti-colonial and socialist movements worldwide would study, copy, and argue over for decades."},{"heading":"Key events & causes","text":"The timeline is sharp. In March 1917, called February by Russia''s old calendar, strikes and mutinies forced Nicholas II to abdicate, ending three centuries of Romanov rule. In November 1917, called October by the old calendar, Lenin''s Bolsheviks overthrew the Provisional Government. In 1918 the new regime signed the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk to exit the world war, ceding vast territory. A civil war from 1918 to 1922 pitted the communist Reds against the loosely allied Whites, with foreign powers intervening; the Reds won through central control and the new Red Army. The Soviet Union was formally created in December 1922. Early hardship under war communism gave way to Lenin''s limited-market New Economic Policy in 1921. Lenin died in 1924, and by the late 1920s Joseph Stalin had outmaneuvered rivals such as Leon Trotsky to take control. Through the Communist International, founded in 1919, Moscow encouraged revolutions abroad, and the Chinese Communist Party, formed in 1921, was one early result."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which revolution of 1917 forced the Tsar to abdicate? 2) Who led the Bolsheviks? 3) What slogan captured Bolshevik promises? 4) In what year was the Soviet Union formally created? 5) Who took control after Lenin''s death in 1924? Answers: 1) The February Revolution, which fell in March by the modern calendar. 2) Vladimir Lenin. 3) Peace, land, and bread. 4) 1922. 5) Joseph Stalin, after defeating rivals including Leon Trotsky. Bonus: name one country where a communist party formed in the early 1920s partly inspired by these events. A good answer notes China, whose Communist Party was founded in 1921 and would reshape Asian history. You might also mention that the Communist International, set up in 1919, deliberately spread the revolutionary model abroad."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: merging the two 1917 revolutions. February toppled the monarchy and produced the Provisional Government; October brought the Bolsheviks to power. Mistake: assuming the revolution was instantly secure. It took a bloody civil war from 1918 to 1922 before Bolshevik rule was safe. Mistake: treating the revolution as a purely Russian story. It created the first communist state and a worldwide movement, shaping decolonization, the Cold War, and politics on every continent. Keep the dates and the global reach straight. Exit ticket: (a) How did a small, disciplined party come to rule the world''s largest country? (b) Name the civil war''s two main sides. Answers: (a) By exploiting wartime collapse, promising peace and land, seizing the capital in October 1917, and then winning the civil war through tight organization and the Red Army. (b) The communist Reds and the anti-communist Whites."}]}'::jsonb, null, 14),
  (5, 'The Great Depression and the rise of fascism', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain how a financial crash became a global depression, and how that economic disaster helped fascist and militarist regimes take power. By the end you will be able to connect the economics of the 1930s to the politics that led toward a second world war. Warm-up: before reading, suggest one way mass unemployment might make extreme political movements more appealing. Keep the global frame: the Depression struck not only the United States and Europe but also economies in Asia, Africa, and Latin America that depended on exporting raw materials. As you read, watch how desperation, fear, and wounded national pride opened doors for leaders promising order and renewal. Hold one question: why did some democracies survive the 1930s while others fell to dictatorship? We return to it in the exit ticket, after seeing how Germany, Italy, Japan, and the Soviet Union each followed a different hard road through the decade."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The Great Depression was the deepest economic collapse of the modern age. It began with the Wall Street Crash of October 1929 and spread worldwide as banks failed, trade shrank, and unemployment soared, reaching a quarter of the workforce in some countries. Because the global economy was linked by trade, debt, and the gold standard, the slump jumped borders fast, hitting raw-material exporters across Asia, Africa, and Latin America as prices collapsed. Politically, the Depression discredited existing leaders and made radical solutions attractive. In several countries this fed fascism, a movement built on extreme nationalism, a cult of the leader, contempt for democracy, and often violent racism. Fascists blamed scapegoats, promised national revival, and crushed opposition. Not every country fell this way: the United States responded with Franklin Roosevelt''s New Deal, a program of public works and reform that kept democracy intact. The decade became a contest between democratic reform, communist command economies, and fascist dictatorship, a contest that would soon turn into war."},{"heading":"Key events & causes","text":"Key developments crowded the 1930s. The 1929 crash triggered bank failures and a collapse in world trade, worsened by protectionist tariffs such as the United States Smoot-Hawley Act of 1930. In Germany the economic ruin shattered the fragile Weimar Republic; Adolf Hitler and the Nazis exploited unemployment, fear of communism, and resentment of the Versailles treaty, and Hitler was appointed chancellor in January 1933. In Italy Benito Mussolini, in power since 1922, tightened his fascist grip. In the Soviet Union Stalin drove rapid industrialization through Five-Year Plans and forced collectivization, which caused a devastating famine, including the Holodomor in Ukraine in 1932 to 1933. Japan, hit hard by the slump, turned to military expansion and invaded Manchuria in 1931. Spain descended into civil war from 1936 to 1939, won by the fascist-backed nationalist Francisco Franco. The common thread was economic desperation turning into political extremism across several continents at once."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What event in 1929 is usually said to start the Depression? 2) Name the United States program that answered the crisis while preserving democracy. 3) In what month and year did Hitler become chancellor? 4) What did Japan invade in 1931? 5) Name the 1932 to 1933 famine tied to Soviet collectivization. Answers: 1) The Wall Street Crash of October 1929. 2) Franklin Roosevelt''s New Deal. 3) January 1933. 4) Manchuria, in northeastern China. 5) The Holodomor in Ukraine. Bonus: in one sentence, link the Depression to the rise of fascism. A strong answer says mass unemployment and fear discredited existing governments and made fascist promises of order, revival, and scapegoats more appealing. You could also note that the Spanish Civil War from 1936 to 1939 became an early battleground between fascism and its opponents."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating the Depression as only a United States event. It was global, striking commodity exporters in Asia, Africa, and Latin America as hard as industrial states. Mistake: assuming economic crisis automatically produces dictatorship. It raises the risk, but choices and institutions matter: the United States and Britain stayed democratic, while Germany did not. Mistake: lumping fascism and communism together as identical. Both were dictatorships, but their ideologies and bases differed sharply, and they were bitter enemies. Keep cause and effect careful: economics set the stage, but politics decided the outcome. Exit ticket: (a) Why did some democracies survive the 1930s while others fell? (b) Name two fascist or militarist regimes that expanded in the decade. Answers: (a) Stronger institutions, flexible reform such as the New Deal, and less severe national grievance helped democracies endure, while deep humiliation and weak institutions helped dictatorship win elsewhere. (b) Any two of Nazi Germany, Fascist Italy, and militarist Japan."}]}'::jsonb, null, 15),
  (6, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The First World War was triggered in 1914 by the assassination in Sarajevo of…","options":["Tsar Nicholas II","Archduke Franz Ferdinand","Kaiser Wilhelm II","Emperor Franz Joseph"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria-Hungary was killed by Gavrilo Princip on 28 June 1914, setting off the chain of alliances."},{"prompt":"Which empire governed roughly a quarter of the world in 1900?","options":["The British Empire","The Ottoman Empire","The Qing Empire","The Austro-Hungarian Empire"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The British Empire controlled about a quarter of the planet''s land and population at the century''s start."},{"prompt":"The Bolsheviks who seized power in October 1917 were led by…","options":["Joseph Stalin","Leon Trotsky","Vladimir Lenin","Alexander Kerensky"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Lenin led the October Revolution; Stalin took control only after Lenin''s death in 1924."},{"prompt":"The Great Depression is usually dated from…","options":["the 1919 Treaty of Versailles","the 1929 Wall Street Crash","the 1933 New Deal","the 1931 invasion of Manchuria"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Wall Street Crash of October 1929 began the global slump of the 1930s."},{"prompt":"Hitler was appointed chancellor of Germany in…","options":["1923","1929","1933","1939"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Hitler became chancellor in January 1933, amid the economic ruin of the Depression and the instability of the Weimar Republic."},{"prompt":"Which four empires collapsed as a result of the First World War?","options":["British, French, Dutch, and Belgian","German, Austro-Hungarian, Russian, and Ottoman","Russian, Qing, Japanese, and British","Ottoman, Spanish, Portuguese, and French"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The war destroyed the German, Austro-Hungarian, Russian, and Ottoman empires, redrawing the map of Europe and the Middle East."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (7, 'World War II and the Holocaust', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain how the Second World War began, why it became the deadliest conflict in history, and what the Holocaust was. By the end you will be able to trace the war''s main turning points on several continents and describe the genocide carried out by Nazi Germany. Warm-up: before reading, name one unresolved problem from the First World War that helped cause the second. Keep the global frame firmly in view: this was a truly worldwide war, fought across Europe, North Africa, the Atlantic, and the vast Asia-Pacific theater, where the conflict began earlier. As you read, hold the scale in mind, roughly 70 to 85 million deaths, most of them civilians. One question to carry through: how did the war in Europe and the war in Asia connect into a single global struggle? We return to it in the exit ticket, after tracing the fighting and the central atrocity of the age."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The Second World War, fought from 1939 to 1945, was a global struggle between the Axis powers, Nazi Germany, Fascist Italy, and militarist Japan, and the Allies, chiefly Britain, the Soviet Union, the United States, and China. Its roots lay in unresolved grievances from the First World War, the Great Depression, aggressive expansion by the Axis, and the failure of other powers to stop that expansion early. The war combined fast mechanized offensives with mass bombing of cities and the total mobilization of whole societies. It was genuinely worldwide: in Asia the fighting had begun with Japan''s war on China, while in Europe it opened with Germany''s conquests. At its heart lay a crime without precedent, the Holocaust, the deliberate, industrialized murder of about six million Jews, along with Roma, disabled people, political prisoners, and others. The war ended with the defeat of the Axis, the dawn of nuclear weapons, and a world soon split between two new superpowers."},{"heading":"Key events & causes","text":"The war in Asia escalated with Japan''s full invasion of China in 1937. In Europe it began on 1 September 1939, when Germany invaded Poland and Britain and France declared war. Germany overran France in 1940 but failed to defeat Britain from the air. In June 1941 Germany invaded the Soviet Union, and in December 1941 Japan attacked Pearl Harbor, bringing the United States fully into the war. The tide turned in 1942 and 1943 at Stalingrad, where the Soviets broke the German advance, and in the Pacific after the Battle of Midway. The Allied landings in Normandy on 6 June 1944 opened a western front in Europe. The Holocaust was coordinated at the Wannsee Conference in 1942 and carried out in death camps such as Auschwitz. Germany surrendered on 8 May 1945. After atomic bombs fell on Hiroshima and Nagasaki, Japan surrendered in August 1945, ending the deadliest war in history."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) On what date did Germany invade Poland? 2) Which event brought the United States fully into the war in 1941? 3) Which 1942 to 1943 battle marked the turning point on the Eastern Front? 4) What was the Holocaust? 5) When did Germany surrender? Answers: 1) 1 September 1939. 2) Japan''s attack on Pearl Harbor in December 1941. 3) The Battle of Stalingrad. 4) The deliberate, industrialized murder of about six million Jews, along with Roma, disabled people, and others, by Nazi Germany. 5) 8 May 1945. Bonus: why is it accurate to call this a world war rather than a European one? A good answer notes that major fighting raged across Asia and the Pacific, Africa, and the oceans, and that the Asian war against Japan began before the European war."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: dating the whole war from 1939. The European war began in 1939, but the Asian war against Japan was already underway, with full-scale invasion of China from 1937. Mistake: underrating the Soviet role. The Eastern Front saw the largest battles and the heaviest losses, and Soviet resistance was decisive in defeating Germany. Mistake: treating the Holocaust as a side event. It was a central aim of Nazi policy and a defining atrocity of the century, demanding careful, accurate memory. Keep the global scope and the human cost in focus. Exit ticket: (a) How did the wars in Europe and Asia connect into one global conflict? (b) Roughly how many people died in the war? Answers: (a) Axis cooperation, worldwide empires, and the entry of powers such as the United States and the Soviet Union linked the theaters, especially once Pearl Harbor joined the Pacific and European wars. (b) About 70 to 85 million."}]}'::jsonb, null, 15),
  (8, 'The atomic age, the Cold War, and the end of empire', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain how nuclear weapons and the Cold War reshaped global politics after 1945, and how dozens of colonies won independence in the same years. By the end you will be able to describe the early Cold War in Europe and the first great wave of decolonization in Asia and Africa. Warm-up: before reading, suggest one reason colonized peoples might expect independence after a war fought partly in the name of freedom. Keep the global frame central: the postwar world was remade not only by the rivalry of two superpowers but by the collapse of European empires and the birth of dozens of new nations. As you read, connect the two stories, since the Cold War and decolonization constantly shaped each other. Hold one question: why did empires that looked so solid in 1900 fall apart so quickly after 1945? We return to it in the exit ticket."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The end of the Second World War opened two linked transformations. First came the atomic age and the Cold War. The United States used atomic bombs in 1945, and when the Soviet Union tested its own in 1949, the world entered a nuclear arms race under the shadow of possible annihilation. The former allies split into rival blocs: a United States-led camp of capitalist democracies and a Soviet-led camp of communist states, divided in Europe by what Winston Churchill called an iron curtain. This rivalry, fought through politics, economics, espionage, and proxy wars rather than direct battle, would last four decades. Second came decolonization. Exhausted European empires could no longer hold their colonies against rising nationalist movements, and the war had shattered the myth of European invincibility. From 1945 onward, country after country in Asia and Africa won independence. The two stories intertwined, as both superpowers courted the new nations, many of which tried to stay non-aligned."},{"heading":"Key events & causes","text":"The early Cold War moved fast. The United Nations was founded in 1945. The Truman Doctrine of 1947 pledged to contain communism, and the Marshall Plan of 1948 rebuilt Western Europe. The Berlin Blockade of 1948 to 1949 was answered by a massive airlift, and in 1949 the Western allies formed NATO while communist forces under Mao Zedong won the Chinese Civil War. The Soviet bloc created the Warsaw Pact in 1955. Decolonization surged alongside: India and Pakistan became independent in 1947, Indonesia from the Dutch in 1949, and Vietnam defeated France at Dien Bien Phu in 1954. Ghana led sub-Saharan Africa to independence in 1957, and 1960 was called the Year of Africa, when seventeen nations became free. Algeria won independence from France in 1962 after a brutal war. At the 1955 Bandung Conference, newly independent states launched what became the Non-Aligned Movement, refusing to line up fully behind either superpower."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In what year did the Soviet Union test its first atomic bomb? 2) What was the Marshall Plan? 3) Which alliance did the Western allies form in 1949? 4) When did India and Pakistan become independent? 5) What was special about 1960 in African history? Answers: 1) 1949. 2) A United States program begun in 1948 to rebuild Western Europe''s economies and resist communism. 3) NATO. 4) 1947. 5) It was the Year of Africa, when seventeen nations gained independence. Bonus: name the movement, launched from the 1955 Bandung Conference, of states refusing to side fully with either superpower. A good answer identifies the Non-Aligned Movement, which gave many newly independent nations a shared voice in a world split between two blocs."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating the Cold War as only a United States and Soviet story. It played out across Asia, Africa, and Latin America, where decolonization and superpower rivalry constantly overlapped. Mistake: imagining decolonization was quick and peaceful everywhere. Some transfers were negotiated, but others, as in Algeria and Vietnam, involved long and bloody wars. Mistake: assuming new nations simply picked a side. Many, through the Non-Aligned Movement, tried to chart an independent course. Keep both transformations in view at once, since they shaped each other. Exit ticket: (a) Why did empires that looked solid in 1900 collapse so fast after 1945? (b) Name the two superpower-led alliances of the early Cold War. Answers: (a) The war drained European powers, discredited claims of European superiority, and energized nationalist movements, while both superpowers opposed old-style empire. (b) NATO and the Warsaw Pact."}]}'::jsonb, null, 15),
  (9, 'A divided world: proxy wars, rights movements, and 1989', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain how the Cold War was fought through proxy conflicts around the globe, how powerful rights and social movements reshaped societies, and how the Cold War ended between 1989 and 1991. By the end you will be able to connect superpower rivalry, struggles for justice, and the sudden collapse of communism in Europe. Warm-up: before reading, name one place far from Europe where the Cold War turned into real fighting. Keep the global frame central: the century''s second half saw proxy wars across Asia, Africa, and Latin America, alongside movements for civil rights, decolonized dignity, and women''s equality that crossed borders. As you read, hold the two threads together, since demands for freedom at home and abroad often echoed one another. One question to carry through: why did the Soviet bloc, which seemed permanent, fall apart so quickly around 1989? We return to it in the exit ticket."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Because direct war between nuclear superpowers risked destroying the world, the Cold War was often fought indirectly, through proxy conflicts in which the United States and the Soviet Union backed opposing sides in other countries. At the same time, the postwar decades saw a global wave of movements demanding rights and dignity: against racial segregation, against colonial and white-minority rule, and for women''s equality. These struggles were connected, as activists learned from one another across borders, and the language of freedom used against fascism and empire was turned toward justice at home. The superpower contest and the rights revolutions ran side by side for decades. Then, with startling speed, the Cold War ended. Economic stagnation, the costs of the arms race, and reforms inside the Soviet Union loosened Moscow''s grip, and in 1989 the communist governments of Eastern Europe fell one after another. By the end of 1991 the Soviet Union itself had dissolved, closing the defining rivalry of the age."},{"heading":"Key events & causes","text":"Proxy and Cold War conflicts spanned the globe: the Korean War from 1950 to 1953, the long Vietnam War, with heavy United States involvement from the mid-1960s until 1973 and the fall of Saigon in 1975, and the Soviet war in Afghanistan from 1979 to 1989. The Cuban Missile Crisis of 1962 brought the world closest to nuclear war. The space race ran alongside, from Sputnik in 1957 to the Apollo 11 Moon landing in 1969. Rights movements reshaped societies: the United States civil rights movement won the Civil Rights Act of 1964, led by figures such as Martin Luther King Jr.; the anti-apartheid struggle in South Africa pressed on until Nelson Mandela''s release in 1990 and the end of apartheid in 1994; and a second wave of feminism spread in the 1960s and 1970s. The end came fast: under Mikhail Gorbachev''s reforms, the Berlin Wall fell on 9 November 1989, and the Soviet Union dissolved in December 1991."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name two Cold War proxy or related conflicts. 2) What 1962 crisis brought the world closest to nuclear war? 3) What major United States law did the civil rights movement win in 1964? 4) When did the Berlin Wall fall? 5) When was the Soviet Union dissolved? Answers: 1) Any two of the Korean War, the Vietnam War, and the Soviet war in Afghanistan. 2) The Cuban Missile Crisis. 3) The Civil Rights Act of 1964. 4) 9 November 1989. 5) December 1991. Bonus: who was the Soviet leader whose reforms, known as glasnost and perestroika, helped bring the Cold War to an end? A good answer names Mikhail Gorbachev, whose openness and restructuring loosened Soviet control over Eastern Europe."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: seeing the Cold War as a quiet standoff. For millions in Korea, Vietnam, Afghanistan, and elsewhere it meant devastating real war. Mistake: treating rights movements as purely national. The United States civil rights struggle, African decolonization, the anti-apartheid cause, and feminism influenced one another across borders. Mistake: assuming the Soviet collapse was inevitable or predicted. Few expected it, and its speed in 1989 to 1991 surprised nearly everyone. Keep the global connections and the suddenness of the ending in mind. Exit ticket: (a) Why did the Soviet bloc fall apart so quickly around 1989? (b) Name one leader of a major rights movement in this period. Answers: (a) Economic stagnation, the burden of the arms race, and Gorbachev''s reforms weakened central control, so once Moscow would not intervene by force, the Eastern European governments collapsed. (b) Martin Luther King Jr. or Nelson Mandela, among others."}]}'::jsonb, null, 15),
  (10, 'Globalization, technology, and the world at century''s end', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain how the end of the Cold War, the spread of new technology, and deepening globalization reshaped the world at the close of the twentieth century. By the end you will be able to describe the economic, technological, and political changes of the century''s final decade and weigh their promise against their problems. Warm-up: before reading, name one technology that changed daily life in the 1990s. Keep the global frame central: the century ended with billions of lives being transformed by trade, computing, and communication, but with the benefits spread very unevenly. As you read, balance optimism and caution, since the 1990s brought both hope for a connected, peaceful world and new worries about inequality and the environment. Hold one question: did globalization at the century''s end bring the world together, divide it further, or both at once? We return to it in the exit ticket."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"With the Cold War over, the 1990s opened what many called a new era of globalization: the deepening flow of goods, money, people, and ideas across borders. Trade barriers fell, multinational companies built supply chains spanning continents, and the World Trade Organization was created in 1995 to manage the new global economy. At the same time a technological revolution took hold. Personal computers became common, mobile phones spread, and the World Wide Web, invented around 1989 to 1991, began to connect the planet in ways never seen before. Politically, the collapse of the Soviet rival left the United States as the single dominant power, while regional blocs such as the European Union, formed by the Maastricht Treaty of 1992, grew stronger. China, reforming its economy since 1978 under Deng Xiaoping, rose rapidly and lifted hundreds of millions out of poverty. The result was a more connected but still deeply unequal world entering a new century."},{"heading":"Key events & causes","text":"Several developments defined the century''s end. Economic globalization accelerated through trade agreements and the founding of the World Trade Organization in 1995. The European Union was established by the Maastricht Treaty of 1992, deepening integration across the continent. China''s market reforms, begun under Deng Xiaoping in 1978, produced extraordinary growth through the 1990s and dramatically reduced poverty. The World Wide Web, created by Tim Berners-Lee around 1989 to 1991, moved from research labs into homes and businesses, and by 2000 the internet was reshaping commerce, media, and communication. Alongside the optimism came warning signs: rising inequality within and between nations, financial crises such as the Asian crisis of 1997, and growing awareness of environmental limits, marked by the Kyoto Protocol on climate change in 1997. World population passed roughly six billion around 2000. The century that had opened with rival empires closed with a single interconnected, if unequal, global system."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What organization, founded in 1995, was created to manage global trade? 2) Which 1992 treaty established the European Union? 3) Who began China''s market reforms, and in what year? 4) Around when was the World Wide Web invented? 5) What 1997 agreement addressed climate change? Answers: 1) The World Trade Organization. 2) The Maastricht Treaty. 3) Deng Xiaoping, beginning in 1978. 4) Around 1989 to 1991. 5) The Kyoto Protocol. Bonus: in one sentence, name both a benefit and a drawback of late-century globalization. A strong answer might note that globalization spread goods, technology, and growth that lifted many from poverty, while also widening inequality and straining the environment."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating globalization as brand new in the 1990s. Trade and migration had linked the world for centuries, but the pace, scale, and technology of the 1990s were unprecedented. Mistake: assuming everyone benefited equally. Growth was real but uneven, lifting hundreds of millions in places such as China while leaving others behind and widening gaps. Mistake: seeing the century''s end as a simple happy ending. The 1990s brought genuine progress alongside financial crises, conflicts, and mounting environmental concern. Keep both the promise and the problems in view. Exit ticket: (a) Did globalization at the century''s end bring the world together, divide it further, or both? (b) Name one technology and one institution that shaped the 1990s. Answers: (a) Both: it connected economies and cultures while deepening inequality and new tensions. (b) Technology such as the World Wide Web or mobile phones, and an institution such as the World Trade Organization or the European Union."}]}'::jsonb, null, 14)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0005-0000-4000-8000-000000000018' and l.position = v.pos
